I dispise the idea of a funeral. I would rather if everyone moved on, I am planning to self-isolate for long enough to make the remaining connections so weak that it would be a blip on the radar, and nothing more. This idea seems to justify the suicide anyways, as it reinforces the collapsing self-esteem. I am not meant to live. The suicide note is simple, just want this death to be of little trouble and / or consequence as possible. There is no need for any system to change, for this was just delayed for 15 years. I should die. I should just do it somehow, with some sort of finality. I am not happy.

### 180726
Many, many many times over the past decade, I have come to the conclusion that I had to die by my own hand. There is no solution other than death. Funnily enough, I did not feel any such thing when in a relationship nor did I feel it when I broke up, I think what I felt then was sadness from the loss and then anger which cooled to stale bitterness. Bitterness, not at what could've been, but more of bitterness of what I overlooked, and what I let slide, and what I worked on that crumbled. Anyways, my understanding of this is that while in a relationship there is a feeling that there is at least one person who would support me if I crashed, which I don't have now.

 I found an old comment on my very very old reddit account, something I stumbled upon when searching this website on google, (note to self update robots.txt to remove from indexer)

> Go to DecidingToBeBetter
> r/DecidingToBeBetter
> •
> 7y ago
> darkwhitemarker
> My parents signed me up for a religious camp, but I'm afraid to go
>
> I don't know whether this allowed on this subreddit. I apologise in advance.
>
> I (17M) am in a religious household and but I don't believe. Up until recently, my parents have been respectful of my wishes to not partake in religion. However my mother upon the suggestion of her religious friend, put my name in a ballot for this camp. Apparently it is very difficult to get into this camp, but I'm afraid because I haven't been religious for a long period of time. My parents have already paid for the camp. I'm not sure whether I want to go and break out of my comfort zone.
>
> How do you guys deal with situations like these when you don't want to be pushed out of your comfort zone?

It is quite interesting, I remember this situation quite clearly. It was when my parents had balloted for an spot in a week-long retreat for young adults. I was pretty afraid, because I had refused to go for Confirmation (which is one of sacraments), and I was afraid that this was a tactic to get me to somehow rope me in and do so? Since the camp was hosted by the arch-diocese and not the local church. I hated the idea as well, it felt like I would be forced to lie and I was just wasting a seat, for someone else who actually wanted to get in but could not because I took their spot. I was a coward as well. In the end, I remember that my parents brought me to them, and I told the lady in charge that I was not participating, and we left. I don't know what happened exactly, though I remember there being an office, and a set of stairs. Maybe my parents had to talk to the lady for longer to save face? I did not care for that. 

It always surprised me how to them, their reputation was so much more important to them than the thoughts and opinions of their own son. Not even validating or agreeing with them, just respectfully accepting them. It was odd. Anyways, I also found this:

> I feel pretty sad too. I feel like I have no one backing me. My entire personality is based on helping other people through their stuff and taking their anger or hatred or whatever sadness they have and just being a sponge to it and like caring for them. And I'm sad cause after doing this for so long, I realise no one really was willing to be that sponge for me and when I have issues I can't turn to anyone for help. I don't know what to do because I have a reputation of being this person that people depend on and I'm scared that if I fail them then they will crash and burn. But life goes on and I live to see another day. I hope you have a great day too!

It seems like whatever I was dealing with then, I am still dealing with a version of that. It has changed, though that idea of me helping people still remains. However, I think what has developed is that it is not necessarily that there is no one who is willing to comfort me, it is that I just do not trust anyone to understand and comfort me anymore. I don't trust anyone anymore. It is bizzare. Trust, trust, trust. I cannot be comfortable around anyone anymore, except myself. This leads to social isolation, which as you have mentioned before, does not do me any favours.
